## Claim
Text: "Wearing face masks can cause infections from bacteria such as staphylococcus."
Claim author: Jane Taylor
Claim date: October 24, 2020
Claim origin: https://archive.ph/IOnJa

## Initial Q&A
### Can wearing face masks cause bacterial infections in the wearer?
Answer: No, wearing face masks does not cause bacterial infections such as staph infections. According to theUSA Today fact-check, both Dr. Zaineb Makhzoumi and Dr. Adam Friedman "confirmed to USA TODAY that face masks do not cause staph infections," with Makhzoumi stating, "In no cases have we seen or have we heard or have we had an association between mask use and staph infection." Friedman noted that while anything that abrades the skin could theoretically allow bacteria to enter, "It's not unique to a mask," and healthcare workers have worn masks for decades without such complications.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/

### Does wearing a face mask increase the risk of infection from staphylococcus bacteria?
Answer: While wearing a face mask can theoretically increase the risk of a staph infection if the mask causes skin irritation or chafing that creates an open wound, the source states that "it is very rare for a person wearing a mask to develop a staph infection as a result of the mask" and that "it is highly unlikely to occur." The claim that masks can cause staphylococcus infections overstates what is, according to Health Desk, a very low-risk scenario.

Source URL: https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19

### What types of face masks (surgical, cloth, N95, or other) does the claim specifically refer to?
Answer: Based on the search result, the claim does not specify any particular type of face mask (surgical, cloth, N95, or otherwise). The USA Today fact-check article refers broadly to "face masks" in general, and the widely shared social media posts containing screenshots of tweets and anecdotes about "mask-related staph infections" also do not distinguish between mask types. The claim is addressed at a general level as applying to face masks overall.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/

### Are there any peer-reviewed scientific studies that demonstrate a causal link between face mask use and staphylococcus infections?
Answer: No, the search result does not identify any peer-reviewed scientific studies demonstrating a causal link between face mask use and staphylococcus infections. Instead, it cites expert opinions from dermatologists (Dr. Zaineb Makhzoumi and Dr. Adam Friedman) who explicitly state that "in no cases have we seen or have we heard or have we had an association between mask use and staph infection" and that masks "are no more likely to lead to an injury or abrasion to the skin that could allow for infection." The article references fact-checks from Lead Stories and the Associated Press but does not point to any peer-reviewed research supporting the claim.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/

### Under what specific conditions, such as prolonged use, reuse without washing, or contamination, might face masks harbor staphylococcus bacteria?
Answer: According to the source, face masks might pose a risk of staphylococcus infection under specific conditions including: when the mask causes skin irritation, chafing, or rawness that leads to an open cut or wound on the face, particularly if the wearer already has "an open lesion or untreated wound on their face," and when masks are not regularly cleaned. The source notes that "staph infections can only occur through a skin opening," so while "it is highly unlikely to occur," prevention measures include "regularly cleaning your mask," "washing your hands rigorously," and "cleaning and bandaging wounds on your skin," as well as using moisturizers to prevent skin irritation that could lead to an open wound.

Source URL: https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19

### What biological mechanism would allow a face mask to cause a staphylococcus infection in the wearer?
Answer: According to the source, the proposed biological mechanism involves the face mask irritating and chafing the skin, which can create a cut, wound, or open lesion on the face; since *Staphylococcus aureus* is a germ commonly found on people's skin, the bacteria could then enter the body through that skin opening, though the source emphasizes this is "very rare" and would require a pre-existing or mask-induced skin opening, explaining that "staph infections can only occur through a skin opening."

Source URL: https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19

### Do staphylococcus bacteria naturally colonize human skin, nostrils, or respiratory tract, and could mask wear simply transfer these existing bacteria rather than cause a new infection?
Answer: Yes, the search result clearly indicates that *Staphylococcus aureus* naturally colonizes human skin and mucosa, with the anterior nares (nostrils) being the primary reservoir. The source states: "Up to 30% of the human population are asymptomatically and permanently colonized with nasal *Staphylococcus aureus*," and that "S. aureus colonizes the anterior nares of 20% to 80% of the human population." Furthermore, "*Staphylococcus aureus* is both a human skin and mucosae commensal," and "S. aureus can be found in different body sites like the skin, rectum, vagina, gastrointestinal tract and axilla, the anterior nares appearing as the main reservoir." This supports the premise that mask wear may simply transfer pre-existing commensal bacteria from the wearer's own skin and nasal passages onto the mask surface, rather than introducing a new infection—any subsequent infection would more likely arise from these normally harmless colonizing bacteria being introduced to a vulnerable site (e.g., a surgical wound), as the source notes regarding endogenous transmission.

Source URL: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmicb.2018.02419

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Insights

The fact-check evidence reveals that the claim "Wearing face masks can cause infections from bacteria such as staphylococcus" is addressed by multiple sources with consistent findings. The USA Today fact-check features two dermatologists (Dr. Zaineb Makhzoumi and Dr. Adam Friedman) who explicitly state that face masks do not cause staph infections, with Makhzoumi noting no association between mask use and staph infection has been observed. The Health Desk source acknowledges a theoretical mechanism—where mask-induced skin irritation could create an opening for staph bacteria (which naturally colonize human skin and nostrils in 20-80% of the population) to enter—but emphasizes this is "very rare" and "highly unlikely to occur." No peer-reviewed studies demonstrate a causal link between face mask use and staphylococcus infections.

## Information Still Lacking

The evidence is sufficient to reach a conclusion. The key question is whether the theoretical mechanism (described as "very rare" and "highly unlikely") is enough to make the claim "technically true" or whether the strong expert refutation makes it "refuted."

## Conclusion

The claim is technically true in a very narrow sense—there is a theoretical mechanism by which mask-induced skin damage could allow staphylococcus bacteria (already present on the skin) to enter through an open wound. However, the claim misleads by presenting this as a general risk without the critical context that: (1) experts explicitly state masks do not cause staph infections, (2) no peer-reviewed evidence supports the claim, (3) the theoretical scenario is described as "very rare" and "highly unlikely," and (4) the risk only exists under specific conditions (pre-existing skin damage, poor mask hygiene). Including this context would create a significantly different impression, as the claim implies a meaningful health concern when the evidence indicates it is not a practical risk.

`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is rated as **cherry-picking** because it presents a theoretical and extremely rare risk as a meaningful health concern. While a theoretical mechanism exists—mask-induced skin irritation could create an opening for *Staphylococcus aureus* (which naturally colonizes the skin and nostrils of 20–80% of the human population, per a [Frontiers in Microbiology](https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmicb.2018.02419) study) to enter the body—two dermatologists interviewed by [USA Today](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/) (Dr. Zaineb Makhzoumi and Dr. Adam Friedman) explicitly confirmed that face masks do not cause staph infections, with Makhzoumi stating no association between mask use and staph infection has been observed. [Health Desk](https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19) acknowledges this theoretical pathway but describes it as "very rare" and "highly unlikely to occur," requiring specific conditions such as pre-existing skin lesions, mask-induced chafing, or poor mask hygiene. No peer-reviewed scientific studies demonstrate a causal link between mask use and staphylococcus infections, and the claim omits this critical context, selectively highlighting the theoretical mechanism while ignoring the strong expert consensus and the rarity of the scenario.